Emphasis on the people.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.820
Right. I know this whole time we were in the bungeling empire.

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:17.920
It's all part of the NES cinematic universe.

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:19.480
Yeah. Okay. Yep.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:25.260
And then a level editor, of course, is included in the game, but there's no way to save levels.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:27.020
So, hooray.

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:30.460
Another one of those. But did anybody play around with that?

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:33.600
Yeah. I dabbled in it a little bit because I do like the level editors.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:39.560
This one, though, I mean, I think I need to say a little bit more about the gameplay as well.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:45.780
But with this level editor, I think it's just that this game is even simpler than Wrecking Crew.

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:51.800
It's not as fun to me to set up the levels because there's not as much to play with to make an interesting puzzle.

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:58.240
Yeah, it's almost like, and it's not as much fun as this, but we were talking about Pac-Man earlier.

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:06.240
Sean mentioned that. And I think that if you were given a level creator for Pac-Man, it's not like, oh my god, sky's the limit.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.000
I want to create so many levels.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:15.640
It's like, you could create a few, and Pac-Man Championship Edition has some great new levels that people haven't played before.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.120
Gimmicky levels. Yeah, gimmicky things.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:22.820
But it's not, at the end of the day, all you're trying to do is just avoid other enemies and pick up gold.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:28.100
In Wrecking Crew, you had a more defined objective that made puzzling cool.

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:31.100
In this one, there's not many puzzles to solve.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:32.300
It's not really a puzzle game.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.240
It's just a maze.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:35.940
Yeah. Wow. Maze is a puzzle.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.120
That's true. I think what...

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:39.360
I didn't... Well, you know what?

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.980
I'm not going to rescind it, but I understand what you're getting at.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:47.420
Are mazes puzzles? Mazes are puzzles in the sense that you have to find your way out of them, but I'm saying

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:48.520
that there's no, like...

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:49.900
It's just A to B.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.720
It's not A to B to C.

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:57.000
When I see, like in an airport, I feel like they say mazes and puzzles, like on the little

00:20:57.680 --> 00:20:58.900
paperback books you can write in.

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:01.260
I feel like it's all...

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.360
They list both as if they're separate entities.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:05.360
Write in if you think a mace is a puzzle.

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:10.720
I don't want to forget that Joe had thoughts on actual gameplay, so I'd like to get back

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:12.060
to those actual thoughts.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:17.200
So, because I actually, while I was playing this, like I said, my initial impression was

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.100
like, I was like, ah, like, great, another one of these.

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.780
But I sort of like reluctantly had fun.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.820
Like, I was kind of like, I wanted to be like bitter about it.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:25.860
I'm like, no, this is stupid.

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:32.580
But I had some fun, and I think that came from like some of the design of the way the

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:37.220
levels work, I think, have to do with the fact that you can't dig straight below you.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:42.560
So like sometimes there'll be like several layers of ground, and if you don't like plan

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:47.560
ahead when you're at the top, how many tiles back you want to go, you're going to end up

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:51.640
trapping yourself where the only place to go is down, and then you can't go down.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:55.180
So like if you don't have anything right next to you, ground right next to you, you can't

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:58.060
dig into a wall, you're trapped.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:21:59.300
Like you have to start the level over.

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:03.180
And I think that was like a little bit of a, what presented a little bit of the challenge.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:07.360
The problem was, was that that was like one of the only things that made it like puzzly

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:10.660
for me. It was like just deciding where to dig those holes.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:11.660
Very one dimensional.

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.380
And that didn't really keep my attention for too long.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:16.980
And I think that that leads to a note that I had kind of going through.

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:21.860
I didn't go through all 50 levels, but as the game progressed, in short, like levels

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:26.920
did get harder in the sense of like enemies were killing me faster, but it wasn't becoming

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:31.080
more challenging from like a, oh, how do I get the gold?

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:32.400
And how do I climb the ladder?

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.680
Like, you know, there was no like new obstacles.

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:41.060
A refresh would have been good if even like every 10 levels, something new got introduced,

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:44.160
you know, because that would at least you'd only need to come up with five new things.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:48.900
And then you'd, you know, like you'd be good to have some fresh gameplay every now and

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.000
again, while maybe like reinventing the hook.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:55.580
With this, it's like, I don't know, after the first 10 levels, I'm sure that there were

00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:59.720
other levels that were harder or maybe like pose some kind of new challenge.

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:00.980
But you already have all the concepts.

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:02.480
Yeah, but all the concepts are down.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.860
And so it just didn't make for a very interesting gameplay.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:09.360
And even, even like beyond just the difficulty based on like adding new concepts, just the

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.620
difficulty based on the design of the puzzles.

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:14.880
I, after a while, I just skipped because you, it's one of those games where you don't have

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:16.960
to play through every level. You can just skip to whatever level you want.

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:19.280
Yeah. I just skipped to level 50 to see what it was all about.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.580
And it wasn't like much harder than some of the other levels.

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:25.340
And in fact, I think I'd probably already played levels that were harder than that one.

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:27.780
I don't remember exactly. Remember, we had that with Wrecking Crew too.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:31.380
Yeah. Which was another game where you could select levels and we kind of were like, oh, the

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.000
last level is not that hard.

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:36.560
It didn't seem like they were trying to do like a gradually getting more difficult, which

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:41.260
makes it. I feel like when you have such a limited palette though, there's, there are only, only so many

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:50.020
permutations of those items or all those instances of obstacle or like, you know, the ground

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:51.120
or ladders again.

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:56.480
Sure, that's true. But I'm saying, I'm saying even without like adding new things or anything like that, if you

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:59.980
just take all those levels and say like, what, there must be an order to which that they're

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:03.140
like generally to most people, like the easiest and the hardest.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:06.780
Yeah. And you like, I don't know, it feels weird to not put them in that order.

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:07.720
Like it's not as rewarding.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:13.860
Yeah. I guess my point was just that it's, I don't think that you can make something all that

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:17.600
much different when you only have, you know what I mean?

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:18.960
Like when there's only so many.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:23.000
Wrecking Crew had so many different like enemies and types of things.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:28.320
And like, you know, the structure of the stages could be designed differently based on like

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:30.140
where you place the bombs and the walls.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:31.940
There was just enough variables.

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:35.460
In this one, to Sean's point, there's just not enough variables in the game to make.

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:37.360
Especially to make 50 levels of it.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:38.920
Maybe you made too many levels.

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:43.080
You could probably just get real gimmicky every now and then as like level variations.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:46.420
Like one with almost no floor and it's all ladders.

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:51.420
All ladders, yeah. All ladders are all ladders and clotheslines, whatever that rope thing is.

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:53.580
You know, but yeah, I agree.

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:55.140
There's really not a lot you can do with it.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:59.840
And I do want to give, since Joe talked about like being able to select the levels, I want

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:01.320
to give that a shout out as a feature.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:02.400
I kind of like that.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:06.800
So this way you don't have to like, let's say someone actually does like this game and

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:10.360
like the level 21 to 29 is just really good.

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.600
You don't want to have to play through all the fat again to get to that.

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:17.100
So I do like that like the games include this as an option.

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:21.700
I'm not saying like Ghost and Goblin should have a stage select right at the beginning,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:23.200
right? That's not like the same comparison.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:26.620
I guess I'm talking more for like Solomon's Key didn't have that.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:30.060
And remember how much we hated that, that like you had to go back to the beginning when

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:33.140
you died, when you actually had an official game over.

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:36.820
I would have liked to have just been able to see all the different variations of the puzzles

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:39.640
because it's kind of like Sudoku, right?

00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:44.320
Like if you do like a Sudoku puzzle, you don't ever want to solve that same puzzle again.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:46.560
You solved it. Now you want a different thing.

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:52.240
So this is a nitpick and I'm not really holding it against the game, but for some reason,

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:58.000
psychologically, like if it's a game like Hitman and as soon as I buy the game, like I

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.040
can play any mission that I want, like that makes sense to me.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:07.960
But if I'm playing an 80s NES game, for some reason, I just feel like I'm in debug mode

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:10.240
if they give me all the levels at once.

00:26:10.540 --> 00:26:11.380
Like, I don't know.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:17.100
Like I feel like the progression of, you know, level one, two, three, four just makes more

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:18.180
sense in that sort of sense.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.880
I understand. It's less rewarding when you have everything.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:21.840
Yeah, exactly. I understand that.

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:23.520
It's kind of like playing with cheat codes on.

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:28.880
Yeah. You know? And it's because like such a large precedent had already been set to say, don't do that.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:34.260
Yeah. Do the, do like the, you know, there's no narrative, but it's almost like if you could

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:38.660
select in Super Mario Brothers to go right to World 8, which you kind of technically can,

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:41.980
but like, let's say even right from the main menu, you would do it.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:45.680
You wouldn't be like, oh, today I'm just going to start on World 3.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:47.260
It's a speedrun. Yeah. Right.

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:53.020
I realized I was going to say something about the AI earlier, and that was just that it's

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:57.340
like really basic, but it is, you can manipulate it, which I think makes good AI.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:00.620
AI. So, I mean, what else is the AI supposed to do in this except for follow you?

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:04.440
So you can kind of lead it down one way and then like juke them and go the other way.

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:10.580
So like I did the first level without digging holes, just because I didn't realize you could

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:11.460
do that the first time I played.

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:17.340
But I did lots of like tricking the AI, which I, it did make me feel at least a little bit

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:19.240
clever. So I think that, you know.

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:20.340
Was it clever or cheesy?

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:22.780
You know, a little bit of both.

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:23.620
But, uh.

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:26.040
Well, I like the juke to your point.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:27.800
Like I do like. You can manipulate them.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:34.280
Which I think is good. Yeah. Doing like sidesteps that make it seem like I'm about to jump off the ledge.

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:35.880
Yeah. And then all the AI like starts jumping.

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:36.960
They're like, guys, he's going to jump.

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:38.060
And they all line up down there.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:39.920
Right. I'm like, you idiots. I'm getting the gold over there.

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:43.760
Exactly. So I thought it was a nice touch because you couldn't necessarily do something like that

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:44.900
in Donkey Kong. Yeah.

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:49.560
Which I guess is like relative to this game since it came out in 83 and Donkey Kong came

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:55.300
out in 81. So there is like some relative programming there that I would give Douglas Smith some credit

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:56.140
for. Just Doug.

00:27:56.540 --> 00:27:58.000
Yeah. I called him Douglas this time.

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:00.640
Mr. Douglas Smith.

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:02.060
Joe, did you think of it?

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:07.380
Yeah. It was just a random, random tack on to what you guys were saying, which about the, I think

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:13.180
the ideal world of these list level, which obviously is limited on the NES.

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:16.440
But like, I think it would be that you're, as you beat a level, you've unlocked it to

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:20.300
play again. But now we're talking about battery saves and whatnot.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.280
Yeah. Like an open world version.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:24.500
Yes. Open world load runner.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:28.300
And I will mention just since we were talking about the stage editor and I missed this.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:35.620
The manual actually does have a cool little like guide for how to create a playable level.

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:41.920
Not that it actually like teaches game theory or design, but just like simple questions

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:43.440
that kids might not think about.

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:47.920
So I thought that that was like a nice touch to have that in the manual just to share the...

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:49.060
Is it possible to complete?

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:50.300
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:53.140
Like are all the gold placed where they can be gotten?

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:56.020
Is there an escape ladder reaching to the top of the screen?

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:58.240
Is there a way to get to the escape ladder?

00:28:58.460 --> 00:28:59.440
You know, things like that.

00:28:59.900 --> 00:29:06.420
That's cool. I think like, you know, we don't need something like that, but kids definitely would need something

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:09.640
like that when they try to make these and then not understand why they're not working

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:13.720
out. Unless they're, you know, really dumb kids, I guess.

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:15.360
Like... And we can't help them.

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:21.660
Yeah, we can't help them. But I just wanted to say that Joe earlier had brought up the arcade game thing and he

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:29.180
wasn't wrong because Irem developed an arcade version of Lode Runner and it added features

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:36.280
like the ability to hang off the ends of ladders and an actual improved enemy AI, but I tried

00:29:36.280 --> 00:29:38.260
to look up like what was improved about it.

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:39.460
Just improved.

00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:45.980
Just improved, yeah. It's like one of those like patch notes that doesn't give you any actual significant details.

00:29:46.340 --> 00:29:47.620
So I was like, all right, whatever.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:48.840
Improved balance.

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:54.800
Yeah, right? And then I'm going to talk a little bit about Doug Smith since...

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:57.160
Dougie! Sean was a little upset I didn't talk about him.

00:29:57.220 --> 00:29:58.260
I read it right at the very top.

00:29:58.460 --> 00:29:59.580
I thought that's what we do here.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:04.820
So I'll give you some quotes from him because we love hearing developer quotes.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:07.080
This is a new segment on the show, the Doug Smith quotes every episode.

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:09.800
Every episode Doug will give his feedback on games.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:15.740
So this was an interview with IGN for like a history of Lode Runner thing.

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:21.720
And I think this actually came out around the time that Lode Runner was getting an N64 game.

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.660
So bear with me for a minute.

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:25.940
Wait, did he develop the N64 game?

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:27.860
I think he played some kind of role in it.

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:29.280
Wow. That's cool.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:35.580
So the interviewer asks, can you talk a little bit about how the original Lode Runner concept

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:36.900
and game came about?

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:42.600
Doug answers, the original Lode Runner game came about in the summer of 1982.

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:48.340
At that point in time, I was a student at the University of Washington studying architecture

00:30:48.340 --> 00:30:50.860
and my minor was in numerical analysis.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:54.920
As a starving student, I took a part-time job at the campus.

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:58.980
That job was a consultant at the Academic Computer Center.

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:04.860
Basically, my job was to help the students log on, print their jobs, etc, etc.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:08.900
But for the summer quarter, there are basically no students.

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:13.380
So I'm sitting at a desk with nothing to do and I had access to all the computers.

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:18.440
So to occupy the time where I was just paid to sit there and do nothing, another friend

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:19.800
and I started to write a game.

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:21.420
We'll pause there for a second.

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:23.180
Does anybody see like a dilemma here?

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:26.720
I think the game is owned by the university.

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:28.840
That's a problem.

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:31.580
Yeah. Thankfully, he didn't admit it until 1999.

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:36.340
And also, well, to be fair, copyrights at the time were probably different.

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:43.100
You know, like, there probably wasn't a precedent for someone making a game at a university

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:47.660
and having it be like a debate for whose intellectual property it is.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:50.840
Yeah, I have no idea how IP law was in the 80s.

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:52.620
So it's fun.

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:58.400
Yeah. And, you know, we mentioned that Broderbund, you know, published the game and stuff like

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:03.140
that. So he submitted a rough version of the game to Broderbund in October of 82.

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:06.480
So it actually really only did take him that summer to make the game.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:11.360
And he received a one-line rejection letter in response.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:15.580
And it was something along the lines of, this is Doug remembering it.

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:18.140
Thank you for submitting your game concept.

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:21.280
Unfortunately, it does not fit within our product line.

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:28.720
And Doug never said no because around Christmas of 1982, he submitted the game, now renamed

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:31.460
to Lode Runner. I don't know what he originally had it renamed as.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:36.560
And he submitted it to four publishers and quickly received offers from all four, Sierra,

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:39.040
Cirrus, Synergistic, and Broderbund.

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:40.640
And he took the deal with Broderbund.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:43.020
Wow. So it turned out all you need is just to change the name.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.940
All you need to do is just change the name and you have a winning product.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:50.580
Wow. Yep. Good job, Doug.

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:54.680
Any thoughts? I thought Doug actually gave like a legitimate answer to an interview.

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:57.240
No, that was very good compared to what we're used to.

00:32:57.320 --> 00:33:00.600
Yeah. I mean, the Japanese talking like, you know, cryptic circle.

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:07.740
Like, I feel like Sakamoto says things and then like people at Nintendo get it.

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:09.160
They're like, oh, yes, yes.

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:11.280
I understand that reference he's making.

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:16.260
And we're all sitting here like, he's just talking about like the smallest little detail

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:17.520
in a game that means nothing.

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:18.800
You missed the bus that day.

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:22.640
Right, right. I have a question, but it's not about the interview.

00:33:22.780 --> 00:33:24.140
It's about the context of the interview.

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:26.520
Sure. Lode Runner for N64?

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:31.120
Yeah. Yeah, we're going to get to that, but it is with 3D polygons.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:35.820
But like a 2.5D kind of deal or is it like a 3D?

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:37.380
Yeah. Let me, hold on.

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:41.460
I could pull it up for just for you guys, but you're going to have to give me.

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:44.220
All right. Well, that was totally different than what I thought it would look like.

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:46.020
Yeah, but you were right about the 2.5D.

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:49.620
Yeah, but it was cool. It was still like in a 3D world, which was cool.

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:51.440
I thought it would be like Super Smash Bros looking.

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:57.680
Yeah, I have a very controversial opinion with that first generation of 3D games for N64

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.580
and PS1 where I hate them all.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:01.300
Oh, I didn't think it was controversial.

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:08.020
I kind of like how crappy they all look, you know, in just like a look where we've come

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:09.500
from kind of deal.

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:12.880
Yeah, I just find most of them to be like, it's actually amazing.

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:19.020
I've kind of fallen in love with like PS1 2D games just because like the sprite work

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:20.380
is like so good.

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:26.280
And it's like, it's like, wow, it's a step above Super Nintendo graphics without like

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:28.920
forgetting all of the good things about Super Nintendo games.

00:34:29.160 --> 00:34:32.300
So it's like every now and again, we need to be reminded that stuff like that exists.

00:34:32.740 --> 00:34:36.280
I'm talking specifically probably about like Castlevania Symphony of the Night, which

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:38.660
is just such a, such a cool game.

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:41.100
Still holds up. Absolutely. That would be on my essential games list.

00:34:41.220 --> 00:34:43.320
I'm just going to say it. Oh, all right.

00:34:43.680 --> 00:34:51.440
Okay. No, I still find, I still find the old PS1 N64 graphics charming in the very least.

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:55.100
And there definitely are games that did it correctly.

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:56.780
Yeah. It really depends on the game.

00:34:56.980 --> 00:34:58.320
I think Metal Gear Solid was.

00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:00.420
Yeah. The closer that they try to look real.

00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:02.160
Yeah. The worse it is.

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:04.200
You guys haven't played. They don't look real yet.

00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:08.360
If it's like a charming like cartoon, then I think like Crash Bandicoot, I think looks

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:12.020
great. This is a tangent, but if you haven't played Paperboy 64.

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:14.040
I have played Paperboy 64.

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:16.200
Just look at that hot, hot garbage.

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:20.020
And it is trash.

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:21.760
That game is not good.

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:25.320
And so that N64 game was called Lode Runner 3D.

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:28.000
In my opinion, it looks archaic.

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:31.520
But, you know, I guess at the time it was received.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:32.760
It was 3D. That one.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.000
Well, I mean, yeah.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:36.860
2.5D was not going to catch on.

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:40.700
That one looked better. If they called it Lode Runner 2.5D, people wouldn't get it.

00:35:40.780 --> 00:35:43.400
That one looked better than I've seen N64 games look.

00:35:43.600 --> 00:35:46.220
So I'll get at that. There were other Lode Runner games though, guys.

00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:48.540
Let's not forget Championship Lode Runner.

00:35:48.720 --> 00:35:50.480
Of course. Which came out in 1984.

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:56.760
A direct sequel with 50 levels edited by fans and intended for expert play.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.280
So fans were so big about it that they made their own levels.

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:02.760
And then this was like the best of the original Lode Runner.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:06.180
Then you have Lode Runner Roman Numeral 2.

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:08.440
Is there something to pronounce it that way?

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:09.420
Are you sure it's not just 11?

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.160
It could be. I think it's I.I.

00:36:12.160 --> 00:36:13.200
It's I.I.

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:17.240
And this was actually on the MSX.

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:19.040
It was only released on the MSX.

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:21.260
And came out in 1985.

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:25.400
22 original levels and 28 new levels.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:26.360
So that's cool.

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:30.680
And sorry, the 22 original levels doesn't mean 22 new.

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:33.060
The first ones. 22 of the first ones, yes.

00:36:33.700 --> 00:36:35.320
It's funny that like for us, right?

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:38.200
Like I don't. I could care less about the levels, right?

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:41.140
Like I'm not like, oh, I hope they're right back stage six.

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:43.960
But apparently fans did.

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:49.500
Then there's Lode Runner's Rescue on the Atari and Commodore 64,

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:50.680
which came out in 1985.

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:53.120
A 3D sequel.

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:55.220
And again, like. People really like this game, huh?

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:56.420
3D not in this.

00:36:56.540 --> 00:36:58.300
You know, it's 3D perspective levels.

00:36:58.300 --> 00:37:03.540
So we're talking about like on the Commodore 64, which couldn't clearly do 3D.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:05.500
That was the name of it like just in Europe, though, right?

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:07.960
Because I think in America it was Lode Runner Other M.

00:37:09.560 --> 00:37:10.760
That's very funny.

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:15.780
Considering we had the Metroid episode.

00:37:15.920 --> 00:37:17.340
That's why I'm laughing so much.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:25.160
But this one also came with a screen design editor and dozens of 3D perspective levels.

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:29.300
So good on them for trying something new with the formula for the first time.

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:35.520
And then Hyper Lode Runner in 1989, which is just the Game Boy version of the game.

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:38.080
So I don't know what's hyper about it.

00:37:38.740 --> 00:37:45.260
Hot. And then Battle Lode Runner, which is not a Battle Royale Lode Runner game.

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:54.060
It's a 1993 PC engine game and revolves around traveling through 50 different stages in 11 different time periods,

00:37:54.740 --> 00:37:56.800
collecting gold without being caught by enemies.

00:37:57.020 --> 00:37:59.880
The characters can climb ropes and ladders.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:01.720
So ladders make their return.

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:04.980
Thankfully, I was worried ladders weren't going to be confirmed.

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:15.000
And you can walk on top of enemies, collect power-ups, and, of course, dig a hole to the right or left of yourself.

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:16.300
They got right and left in there?

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:19.140
Right and left. I'm just imagining these things written on the back of the box.

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:22.340
You can walk on top of enemies in this game, too, don't you?

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:23.620
I don't know. I don't know if you can.

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:24.540
I never tried it.

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:27.960
If they fall in the hole, don't they die, though?

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:31.460
No, if they fall in the hole, they only die if the hole regenerates.

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:33.840
Oh, right, right. So you can use them and you can leave.

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:35.280
Okay, that makes sense. It's like you get them out of your way.

00:38:35.400 --> 00:38:36.520
They fall down and then you walk over.

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:37.920
Right, but then you can walk over. Okay, cool.

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:41.380
I cannot believe that we've been talking about this game for this long.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:44.720
Well, if you wanted more information about Lode Runner,

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:50.500
you could always check the Lode Runner Museum at loadrunner.home.insightbb.com.

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:51.800
I thought it was somewhere in New York.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.040
The Lode Runner Museum in Paris.

00:38:55.580 --> 00:39:00.240
It's funny because we had, like, the elevator museum when we were talking about elevator action.

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:04.520
True. So Lode Runner having a real-life museum wasn't out of the realm of the possibilities.

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:20.320
But before we get to the essential games list, I do need to talk about the reception from media outlets at the time of Lode Runner's release.

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:25.920
Because, as I mentioned, this was a very well-received game and obviously, like, fans loved it.

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:30.940
So it wouldn't be fair if we just kind of poo-poo all over it without talking about how it was received at the time.

00:39:31.120 --> 00:39:37.760
I think it's totally fair. So I'll just give you a couple of, I'll give you the name of the magazine or publisher or whatever, and then what they said.

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:40.360
Wait, to be clear, this is the Apple II version?

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:42.740
This is for, yeah, mostly for the Apple II version.

00:39:42.860 --> 00:39:44.820
I think there's some for the Commodore 64 version.

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:47.040
Okay, but this is, like, how it's meant to be played.

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:48.320
Yes, exactly. Okay.

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:54.680
Softline in 1983 praised Lode Runner, calling it smooth, thoughtful, and quite addictive.

00:39:55.920 --> 00:40:07.840
Great. Computer Gaming World praised Lode Runner's unusually easy-to-use level editor and the strategy necessary for an arcade title, describing it as one of the few thinking men's arcade games.

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:08.860
Oh, my God.

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:12.760
This was a lot earlier than when the NES game came out, correct?

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:23.380
Yes, 83. Ahoy, in 1984, called the game a top-notch action game that requires both a quick mind and an agile joystick.

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:32.800
Zzap 64, called the Commodore 64 version, not one of the most recent games, but certainly one of the best.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:35.320
A classic for a long time to come.

00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:44.540
Graphically minuscule and orally crude, the game's sheer addiction kept my eyes popped open until the owls went to bed.

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:47.840
Until the owls went to bed.

00:40:48.040 --> 00:40:48.900
That is beautifully written.

